\section{Introduction}
Dampelas (ISO 639-3 \texttt{dms}; also Dian, Dampal, Dampelasa) is an Austronesian language of Central Sulawesi, Indonesia, in the Tomini-Tolitoli group. Estimates of speakers range from about 2,000 to about 9,500. Sources describe it as threatened, with younger people shifting to Indonesian, and report no literacy in it.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers, textbooks or school programmes for Dampelas were found in search results.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Overview of the language: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dampelas_language}
\item A sketch grammar by Francesca Moro (Leiden University master's thesis, 2010) was mentioned in search summaries as the main grammatical description; no verified direct URL for it was seen, so none is given.
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Joshua Project profile of the Dampelas people: \url{https://joshuaproject.net/people_groups/11484}
\end{itemize}

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Endangered Languages Project entry: \url{https://www.endangeredlanguages.com/lang/1412}
\end{itemize}

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
The language is not known to be taught in schools and has no established literacy materials. A curriculum would depend on new orthography and materials development with the community, drawing on the sketch grammar for linguistic background.

\end{document}
